    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "The covariance is a measure of association between $x$ and $y$ (Figure 3.1). It is positive if $y$ increases with increasing $x$, negative if $y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and zero if there is no linear tendency for $y$ to change with $x$. If $x$ and $y$ are independent, then $\\sigma(x,y) = 0$, but the converse is not true — a covariance of zero does not necessarily imply independence. (We will return to this shortly; see Figure 3.3.)",
      "source_page": 53,
      "printed_page": 33,
